{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261124T001828Z-7af31032", "ts": "2026-11-24T00:21:37Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "fix/free-interval-warnings", "build": "ecd39ffbf786"}, "body": {"base_commit": "b3cce748e06c5b5980a960d62c70c76a817546b9", "base_tree": "fdff744b5c5e86b98994d4b40300ba52ab1cbfad", "head_tree": "117d1e2af766803d03d836107396c9186cc6e31c", "head_commit": "e534f9c19b09ceeda2a89cf7e7c4e32f5c46d339", "dispatch_commit": "e534f9c19b09ceeda2a89cf7e7c4e32f5c46d339", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "tests/test_critic_dispatch_refusal.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "tests/test_critic_dispatch_refusal.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The free-fix remedy is spelled `disposition --fixed --paths`, a flag shape the CLI does not have", "recommendation": "Class, scope bounded to this change's prose. Why it broke: the spelling was copied from the design doc's research paragraph (`documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md:58`, pre-existing) without being re-derived from the parser (authoring rule: 'A claim copied from a record ... inherits its derivation, not its author: re-derive it from code'). `dispositions.disposition_cmd` takes `--fixed <path>[,<path>...]` (its `_RECORD_USAGE`); `--fixed --paths` consumes the literal `--paths` as the path value and then fails on the positional count. Fix both added sites to the real shape, `prawduct-hook disposition <review-id> <fid> --fixed <path>[,<path>...]`, or name the owner of the wording (`dispositions.py:1144`'s `disposition --fixed <paths>`). m2 is the consumer-facing instance, a wrong command in a consumer note that ships to the changelog; it is an oracle file, so the Records Pass rates it, and under Goal 4 a wrong command in an instruction is BLOCKING-grade there.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "Prose this change added that names the command recording a fixed finding with no review round.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py:4149 (begin_review pending_blocking comment)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": ".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md Consumer note (oracle; Records Pass)"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Two counters for one question: `actionable`'s warning term now decides no dispatch, and its comment credits it with keeping judgeable fixes reviewed", "recommendation": "Instance. `delta` (line 4405) and `files_changed` (line 4543) are the same `tree_diff(base_tree, head_tree)`, so after this change the warning term in `actionable` changes exactly one outcome: an EMPTY delta after a warnings-only prior skips the `critic-dispatch-nothing-to-verify` exit and falls through to the free-interval refusal with `free_files: []`, recorded under `critic-dispatch-free-interval`. That is the guard whose yield question ('did it ever refuse a round that turned out to be needed?') `stats.by_guard` counts, so it gets fed no-op events that belong to its sibling. A judgeable fix after a warnings-only review is dispatched by the judgeable conjunct (`not judgeable_files(files_changed)` is false), not by the warning term, so the new comment 'Warnings still count as something to VERIFY ... a warning fixed in judgeable code is reviewed as before' attributes the behaviour to the wrong mechanism and invites a maintainer to keep both counters. Simpler: one blocking-only counter (`actionable` = `pending_blocking`), used by both exits, with the nothing-to-verify reason's 'no blocking/warning findings' narrowed to 'no blocking findings'. The new control test (`test_a_judgeable_fix_after_a_warnings_only_review_still_dispatches`) still pins the judgeable case.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The free-fix route the change hands warnings to is spelled as a command that does not parse: `disposition --fixed --paths`, five sites, one of them a shipping Consumer note", "recommendation": "Records Pass, bar 1 (it ships): `.prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md`'s Consumer note tells consumers to 'Record the fixed warnings with `prawduct-hook disposition --fixed --paths`', and `change_fragments.render` copies that note into the release archive verbatim. `dispositions.py`'s parser has no `--paths` flag. `--fixed` consumes the next token as its value, so `--fixed --paths` records the path list ['--paths'], and with no `<review-id> <fid>` positionals the call exits 2 with usage. The real form is `prawduct-hook disposition <review-id> <fid> --fixed <path>[,<path>...]`, as `review-cycle.md` and the census line in `dispositions.py` print it. I rate this WARNING, not bar 3's BLOCKING. The wrong command fails loudly, and its usage text prints the correct form, so following it leads to a retry rather than a wrong action that succeeds. It also ships only when the release is cut, not at this merge. Scope: class. Why it broke: the design doc's section-1 shorthand was copied into the code comment, the test docstring and the change record without being checked against the parser. 'A claim copied from a record ... inherits its derivation, not its author: re-derive it from code' (authoring learnings). Fix: replace all five sites with the parser's real form, or with the relational 'records it as a FIXED disposition naming the paths (`review-cycle.md`)'. The Consumer note should carry the full command with its positionals, since a consumer runs it as written.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md", "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_dispatch_refusal.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "Any text at e534f9c1 that names the free-fix disposition as `disposition --fixed --paths` (git grep 'fixed --paths').", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": ".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md:21 (Consumer note)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": ".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md:14 (narrative)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md:61"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py:4149 (begin_review comment on pending_blocking)"}, {"id": "m5", "site": "tests/test_critic_dispatch_refusal.py:424 (test_free_interval_after_a_warnings_only_review_is_refused docstring)"}], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "When a verify-resolutions pass that used to record open warnings is refused, the exit-3 output says 'Nothing to do' and never mentions the warnings or how to record them", "recommendation": "Before this change, a verify-resolutions pass over a records-only fix after a warnings-only review ran, and its resolution facts recorded the warnings as fixed. Now `begin_review` refuses that pass with exit 3, and the CLI prints its free-interval reason plus 'Nothing to do: the coverage gate already passes this interval.' SKILL.md step 4, edited in this diff, tells the agent to 'stop: do not re-dispatch'. Neither names the prior review's open warnings or the FIXED disposition that now has to record them. That remedy appears only in a `begin_review` comment, the change record and `review-cycle.md`'s FIX bullet. Consequence: the builder does exactly what the output says and stops. The warnings they fixed stay `undispositioned` in the briefing and the disposition census, and telemetry undercounts fixes, for exactly the case this change creates. 'Prose that REPLACES a deleted control is load-bearing logic' (hook-surface learnings). Fix: when the free-interval refusal fires on `verify-resolutions` and the prior's warning count is nonzero, put the count and the full `disposition <review-id> <fid> --fixed <path>[,...]` route in the result, so the CLI prints it as the last line, and pin it with an assertion in `test_free_interval_after_a_warnings_only_review_is_refused`. Scope: instance. The predicate and the refusal message live in one function, and no other refusal arm gained this case.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "tests/test_critic_dispatch_refusal.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The free-interval refusal arm of begin_review when the prior review has open warnings.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py begin_review free-interval refusal (`critic-dispatch-free-interval` return)"}], "fid": "R-4"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 4, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The free warning-fix remedy is cited as `disposition --fixed --paths`, which is not an invocation the CLI accepts", "recommendation": "The real form is `prawduct-hook disposition <review-id> <fid> --fixed <path>[,<path>...]` (dispositions.py _RECORD_USAGE, disposition_cmd): there is no `--paths` flag, and `--fixed --paths` parses `--paths` as the path value with no positional ids, which gives a usage error (exit 2). Instance, not class: the wording came from design-doc line 57 and was copied into the begin_review comment (critic_consolidate.py ~4149), the new test's docstring, and the oracle change record's Consumer note, which ships. Fix the subject sites by using the real form. The Consumer note is the Records Pass's to rate. The error corrects itself, because the usage line prints the right form, so this is wording and not behaviour.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_dispatch_refusal.py", "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Two sibling descriptions of exit 3 still say 'no finding this mode could resolve'", "recommendation": "SKILL.md's exit-3 sentence was narrowed to 'no blocker this mode could resolve', but the same claim survives in `begin_review`'s docstring (`plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py:3893`) and in the `critic-begin` CLI docstring (`plugin/bin/prawduct-hook:2261`). Docstring wording, so an observation; no test or gate reads either. Core rule: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees: search for the claim, not the tokens you edited'. If these are touched for another reason, make them say 'blocker'.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Framework checks on the SKILL.md edit: clean", "recommendation": "Generality, Instruction Clarity and Cumulative Health: the one-word narrowing ('finding' to 'blocker') keeps the instruction imperative and unambiguous, and does not contradict the consolidation close directive, which already routes a round-free fix through `disposition --fixed <paths>`. Pipeline Coverage does not apply, since no artifact, dimension or Critic check was added. No edit wanted.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The free-interval refusal record cannot tell the new arm (warnings were open) from a refusal over a clean prior", "recommendation": "The `critic-dispatch-free-interval` record exists to answer 'did it ever refuse a round that turned out to be needed?'. Its comment explains that `excluded_wip` was added because a refusal that left work out is a different event. A refusal with prior warnings open is the same kind of distinct event, and it is the one decision 6's own caveat ('A repo where half the findings concern records may differ') would want counted. The record carries no pending-warning count. The count can still be recovered by joining the record's base_tree against the prior review fact in the store, so nothing is lost; recording it on the record would make the count a direct query. No edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Goal 5: the departure from 'warnings are effectively blocking' is recorded and owner-ruled", "recommendation": "The narrowing traces to decision 6 of `documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md`, which flagged it as touching that stance and asked for a ruling. The owner ruled it on 2026-11-23 in `.prawduct/owner-rulings.md`, and the change record names the consumer-visible exit-code change. Grepping the old and new variable names found no reader of the predicate outside `begin_review`. No action needed.", "files": [".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass coverage: both files_oracle members were rated", "recommendation": "I rated `.prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md` and `.prawduct/owner-rulings.md` against the three bars. The change record's wrong command clears bar 1 and is filed above. The owner-rulings entry names `pending_actionable`, which this diff renamed `pending_blocking`. That entry is a dated record of the ruling, and the change record and comment carry the current name, so it clears no bar. Nothing else in either file is false.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "oid": "O-6"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/free-interval-warnings`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "b3cce748e06c5b5980a960d62c70c76a817546b9", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/free-interval-warnings.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-24T00:18:32Z"}}

